he prior year\nFIG 4 revenue change +88.04% estimate party Zacks Consensus Estimates basis from the prior year\nSTAT ev-3 party Zacks Consensus Estimates date 2026-09-28 summary Zacks reports that its Consensus EPS estimate for NVIDIA shifted 4.73% upward over the past month. links none\nFIG 1 Zacks Consensus EPS estimate revision 4.73% upward estimate party Zacks basis None\nANSWER B04 ARTICLE 10\nSTAT e1 party NVIDIA date None summary On NVIDIA's fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch. links [{'question': 'clean-4', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Inventory building to $32 billion ahead of the Vera Rubin launch indicates NVIDIA was stocking supply for the platform's ramp in fiscal Q2 2027; it supports preparation for the launch but does not by itself show volume customer shipments or first revenue recognition.\"}]\nFIG 1 inventory $32 billion actual party NVIDIA basis None\nSTAT e2 party NVIDIA date None summary NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, while the consensus forecast for that quarter was $111.3 billion, above the top of the guided range. links none\nFIG 1 revenue guidance $108 billion guidance party NVIDIA management basis plus or minus 2%\nFIG 2 consensus revenue forecast $111.3 billion estimate party consensus basis above the top of NVIDIA's guided range\nSTAT e3 party NVIDIA date None summary NVIDIA management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue was $96 billion, more than doubled from a year earlier, with growth speeding up for the fourth consecutive quarter. links none\nFIG 1 revenue $96 billion actual party NVIDIA basis more than doubled from a year earlier; fourth consecutive quarter of accelerating growth\nSTAT e4 party NVIDIA date None summary NVIDIA management guided gross margin for fiscal Q3 2027 to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, citing higher component costs, especially memory. links none\nFIG 1 gross margin guidance 74% guidance party NVIDIA management basis plus or minus half a point; cited higher component costs, especially memory\nFIG 2 gross margin 75% actual party NVIDIA basis None\nSTAT e6 party NVIDIA date None summary NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. links [{'question': 'clean-4', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Management's expectation that Vera Rubin will contribute about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027 indicates the platform is expected to be ramping into revenue within fiscal 2027, informing the shipment/revenue-recognition question; it is a forecast, not confirmed volume shipments.\"}]\nFIG 1 Vera Rubin share of data center revenue about 20% guidance party NVIDIA management basis share of data center revenue\nSTAT e7 party Trefis date None summary According to Trefis's analysis, NVIDIA's operating margin is 65%, its highest in ten years, compared with a three-year average of 59%. links none\nFIG 1 operating margin 65% actual party Trefis basis highest in ten years\nFIG 2 three-year average operating margin 59% actual party Trefis basis None\nANSWER B07 ARTICLE 9\nSTAT ev-1 party Zacks date 2026-09-28 summary Zacks Consensus estimates project NVIDIA's upcoming quarterly earnings per share at $2.47, a 90.00% increase versus the same quarter a year earlier, and revenue at $109.02 billion, a 91.24% increase versus the year-ago quarter; the quarter is not identified in the item. links none\nFIG 1 EPS $2.47 estimate party Zacks Consensus basis compared to the same quarter of the previous year (+90.00%)\nFIG 2 revenue $109.02 billion estimate party Zacks Consensus basis compared to the year-ago quarter (+91.24%)\nSTAT ev-2 party Zacks date 2026-09-28 summary Zacks Consensus Estimates project NVIDIA's full-year earnings at $9.25 per share and revenue at $406.05 billion, representing changes of +93.92% and +88.04%, respectively, from the prior year; the item does not state which fiscal year this covers. links none\nFIG 1 earnings per share $9.25 estimate party Zacks Consensus basis change of +93.92% from the prior year\nFIG 2 revenue $406.05 billion estimate party Zacks Consensus basis change of +88.04% from the prior year\nSTAT ev-3 party Zacks date 2026-09-28 summary The Zacks Consensus EPS estimate for NVIDIA shifted 4.73% upward over the past month, per Zacks' own consensus tracking, and NVIDIA carried a Zacks Rank of #1 (Strong Buy) at the time of the article. links none\nFIG 1 Zacks Consensus EPS estimate revision 4.73% upward estimate party Zacks basis None\nANSWER B09 ARTICLE 9\nSTAT e1 party Zacks Investment Research date 2026-09-28 summary Zacks Consensus Estimates project NVIDIA's upcoming quarter earnings per share at $2.47, a 90.00% increase versus the same quarter a year earlier, and revenue at $109.02 billion, a 91.24% increase versus the year-ago quarter. The quarter is not named and no fiscal year is stated in the item. links none\nFIG 1 EPS $2.47 estimate party Zacks Consensus Estimates basis vs. same quarter of the previous year (+90.00%)\nFIG 2 revenue $109.02 billion estimate party Zacks Consensus Estimates basis vs. year-ago quarter (+91.24%)\nSTAT e2 party Zacks Investment Research date 2026-09-28 summary Zacks Consensus Estimates project NVIDIA's full-year earnings at $9.25 per share and revenue at $406.05 billion, representing increases of 93.92% and 88.04% respectively from the prior year. The item does not state which fiscal year the full-year figures cover. links none\nFIG 1 earnings per share $9.25 estimate party Zacks Consensus Estimates basis vs. prior year (+93.92%)\nFIG 2 revenue $406.05 billion estimate party Zacks Consensus Estimates basis vs. prior year (+88.04%)\nSTAT e3 party Zacks Investment Research date 2026-09-28 summary The Zacks Consensus EPS estimate for NVIDIA shifted 4.73% upward over the past month, an estimate revision Zacks cites as reflecting near-term business trends. links none\nFIG 1 Zacks Consensus EPS estimate revision 4.73% estimate party Zacks Consensus Estimates basis upward revision; EPS period not stated\nANSWER B10 ARTICLE 9\nSTAT ev-1 party Zacks Investment Research (consensus estimate) date 2026-09-28 summary Zacks' latest consensus estimate projects Nvidia's upcoming quarterly EPS at $2.47, a 90.00% increase over the same quarter a year earlier, and revenue at $109.02 billion, up 91.24% year over year, for the quarter Nvidia is next due to report. links none\nFIG 1 EPS $2.47 estimate party Zacks Investment Research (consensus estimate) basis consensus projection; +90.00% versus the same quarter of the previous year\nFIG 2 Revenue $109.02 billion estimate party Zacks Investment Research (consensus estimate) basis latest Zacks consensus estimate; +91.24% versus the year-ago quarter\nSTAT ev-2 party Zacks Investment Research (consensus estimate) date 2026-09-28 summary Zacks Consensus Estimates project Nvidia's full-year earnings at $9.25 per share and revenue at $406.05 billion, representing increases of 93.92% and 88.04% respectively from the prior year. links none\nFIG 1 Earnings per share $9.25 estimate party Zacks Investment Research (consensus estimate) basis Zacks Consensus Estimate; +93.92% versus the prior year\nFIG 2 Revenue $406.05 billion estimate party Zacks Investment Research (consensus estimate) basis Zacks Consensus Estimate; +88.04% versus the prior year\nSTAT ev-3 party Zacks Investment Research date 2026-09-28 summary The Zacks Consensus EPS estimate for Nvidia shifted 4.73% upward over the past month, a revision Zacks says reflects analysts' more positive near-term view of the business. links none\nFIG 1 Zacks Consensus EPS estimate revision 4.73% estimate party Zacks Investment Research basis upward shift in the consensus EPS estimate over the past month\nANSWER B12 ARTICLE 4\nSTAT e1 party Wall Street consensus date None summary Wall Street consensus, as reported by Motley Fool, expects Nvidia to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year (the article later ties 'next year's earnings' to fiscal 2028, which ends in January 2028). links none\nFIG 1 revenue growth 91% estimate party Wall Street consensus basis None\nFIG 2 revenue growth 66% estimate party Wall Street consensus basis None\nSTAT e2 party Wall Street consensus date None summary Nvidia is expected by consensus to report earnings per share of $15.68 in fiscal 2028, which the article says ends in January 2028. links none\nFIG 1 earnings per share $15.68 estimate party Wall Street consensus basis None\nANSWER B18 ARTICLE 5\nSTAT e1 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia reported revenue of $96.2 billion for its fiscal second quarter, 106% higher than the same period a year earlier, with a 75% gross margin. links none\nFIG 1 revenue $96.2 billion actual party Nvidia basis company-wide\nFIG 2 revenue growth 106% actual party Nvidia basis year-over-year\nFIG 3 gross margin 75% actual party Nvidia basis company-wide\nSTAT e2 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia guided to $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth. links none\nFIG 1 revenue guidance $108 billion guidance party Nvidia basis company guidance\nFIG 2 sequential revenue growth 17% guidance party Nvidia basis sequential, derived from company guidance\nSTAT e3 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year. links none\nFIG 1 revenue growth guidance roughly 70% guidance party Nvidia basis company guidance\nSTAT e4 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months, with receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July; the article calculates days sales outstanding rose from 45 to 60 days in one quarter. links [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': 'Shows the balance-sheet scale of customer financing: receivables and days sales outstanding rose as Nvidia extended payment terms, informing the total credit-support question; these are not guarantee amounts.'}]\nFIG 1 total assets growth more than 50% actual party Nvidia basis company-wide\nFIG 2 receivables $38.5 billion actual party Nvidia basis company-wide\nFIG 3 receivables $63.1 billion actual party Nvidia basis company-wide\nFIG 4 days sales outstanding 45 to 60 days actual party Nvidia basis company-wide; calculated by article\nSTAT e6 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI. links [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Directly informs the thesis's tracked amount of credit support and guarantees to AI labs/cloud partners: Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of OpenAI data center lease obligations.\"}]\nFIG 1 data center lease guarantee $105 billion actual party Nvidia basis up to; for OpenAI data center lease obligations\nANSWER B20 ARTICLE 5\nSTAT e1 party Nvidia date recently summary Nvidia reported $96.2 billion of revenue for its fiscal second quarter, 106% more than in the same period a year earlier, at a 75% gross margin, according to the article's account of the company's recently reported quarter. links none\nFIG 1 revenue $96.2 billion actual party Nvidia basis company total revenue\nFIG 2 year-over-year revenue growth 106% actual party Nvidia basis versus same period last year\nFIG 3 gross margin 75% actual party Nvidia basis None\nSTAT e2 party Nvidia date recently summary Nvidia's management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year, the article reports. links none\nFIG 1 revenue growth roughly 70% guidance party Nvidia management basis None\nSTAT e3 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for its current quarter, which the article says represents 17% sequential growth. links none\nFIG 1 revenue $108 billion guidance party Nvidia management basis None\nFIG 2 sequential revenue growth 17% guidance party fool.com (article author's calculation from the guidance) basis sequential, versus the reported fiscal second quarter\nSTAT e4 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months, driven in large part by receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July; the article calculates that days sales outstanding rose from 45 to 60 days in one quarter, meaning customers are taking longer to pay. links [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Receivables growth and the rise in days sales outstanding are the working-capital counterpart of Nvidia helping finance customers' orders; the article links part of it to extended payment terms (separate item). It is not itself a guarantee or a credit-support commitment counted toward the thesis's disclosed-amount outcome, but it informs how the support is showing up on the balance sheet.\"}]\nFIG 1 total assets growth more than 50% actual party Nvidia basis company balance sheet\nFIG 2 receivables $38.5 billion actual party Nvidia basis None\nFIG 3 receivables $63.1 billion actual party Nvidia basis None\nFIG 4 days sales outstanding (DSO) 45 to 60 days actual party fool.com (article author's calculation) basis average collection period calculated from reported figures\nANSWER B22 ARTICLE 5\nSTAT e1 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia reported $96.2 billion of revenue for its fiscal second quarter, 106% more than in the same period a year earlier, at a 75% gross margin; the article says revenue and earnings handily beat expectations. links none\nFIG 1 revenue $96.2 billion actual party Nvidia basis None\nFIG 2 revenue growth 106% actual party Nvidia basis growth over the same period last year\nFIG 3 gross margin 75% actual party Nvidia basis None\nSTAT e2 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia guided investors to $108 billion of revenue for the current quarter, which it said represents 17% sequential growth. links none\nFIG 1 revenue guidance $108 billion guidance party Nvidia basis None\nFIG 2 sequential revenue growth 17% guidance party Nvidia basis sequential growth\nSTAT e3 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year. links none\nFIG 1 revenue growth guidance 70% guidance party Nvidia basis roughly\nSTAT e5 party Nvidia date None summary Nvidia's receivables rose from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July, as total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months, and days sales outstanding rose from 45 to 60 days in one quarter; Nvidia said it extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers. links [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Shows NVIDIA financing customers' purchases through extended payment terms on large orders, alongside the formal guarantees the thesis tracks; the receivables jump and DSO rise of 45 to 60 days indicate the scale of that customer credit exposure, but the item gives no guarantee amounts.\"}]\nFIG 1 accounts receivable $38.5 billion actual party Nvidia basis None\nFIG 2 accounts receivable $63.1 billion actual party Nvidia basis None\nFIG 3 days sales outstanding 60 days actual party Nvidia basis up from 45 days\nANSWER B23 ARTICLE 4\nSTAT ev-1 party consensus date None summary Motley Fool contributor Jeremy Bowman reported that Wall Street expects Nvidia to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year, and that Nvidia is expected to earn $15.68 per share in fiscal 2028, which the article says ends in January of that year. These are third-party/consensus forecasts, not company guidance; the article gives no period year for the two revenue growth rates beyond 'this year' and 'next year'. links none\nFIG 1 revenue growth 91% estimate party consensus basis company revenue growth, attributed to Wall Street expectations\nFIG 2 revenue growth 66% estimate party consensus basis company revenue growth, attributed to Wall Street expectations\nFIG 3 earnings per share $15.68 estimate party consensus basis expected EPS, source not named beyond 'expected'\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
